{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on the probability of obtaining a favorable outcome, assuming outcome differences are negligible. First, we identify the possible outcomes and their probabilities. Lottery A has a 25% chance of 6000€ and 75% chance of 0€, so the probability of a favorable outcome (non-zero) is 25%. Lottery B has a 25% chance of 4000€, 25% chance of 2000€, and 50% chance of 0€. Thus, the total probability of a favorable (non-zero) outcome in B is 25% + 25% = 50%. Since the reason treats outcome magnitudes as negligible, it only compares the total probability of winning something. Lottery B has a higher probability (50%) of a favorable outcome than A (25%), so the reason would prefer Lottery B.",
